  "response": "\nThis is a classic problem known as \"The 12 Balls Problem.\" Here's how you can solve it:\n\nStep 1: Use the balance scale twice to find the average weight of the 12 balls. This will give you a rough estimate of the weight of each ball.\n\nStep 2: Use the balance scale once more to compare the weight of 3 of the balls against each other. If two of the balls are the same weight, they are probably the oddball and can be discarded from further consideration. If the third ball is heavier than the other two, it is the oddball and is heavier than the others. If the third ball is lighter than the other two, it is the oddball and is lighter than the others.\n\nStep 3: Now that you know whether the oddball is heavier or lighter, use the balance scale once more to compare the weight of the remaining 9 balls against each other. If two of the balls are the same weight, they are probably the oddball and can be discarded from further consideration. If the third ball is heavier than the other two, it is the oddball and is heavier than the others. If the third ball is lighter than the other two, it is the oddball and is lighter than the others.\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to identify the oddball and determine whether it is heavier or lighter than the other balls. Good luck!",
